WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.530
The full tree sees the original
forty five binary calendar columns.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.690
A path can test an hour and then ask
about working day status or month.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.090
Its reached leaf is the
intersection of those conditions.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.160
This lets the tree express conditional
relationships without us adding every

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.650
product feature explicitly.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.720
Each extra partition also divides the
available training examples into smaller

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:25.910
supporting groups.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.160
The practical question is whether that
extra historical detail improves the later

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.170
forecasts enough to justify it,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.470
rather than whether a larger
model can lower training error.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.780
The source declares depth,

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.640
minimum leaf support and a maximum of
one thousand and twenty four leaves.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.320
The library fits only
training rows and targets.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.420
Our first setting uses depth
three and support twenty.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.180
The second uses depth
eight and support ten.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:01.230
The third removes the depth
limit and uses support one,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.660
while retaining the leaf count cap.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:07.560
A sequence of one hot
questions can become long,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.080
so depth does not simply count
the number of calendar variables.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.920
These structural controls are
the actual fitting limits,

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.100
with no validation early stopping.

00:01:19.275 --> 00:01:24.975
Making the bounded tree more flexible
lowers training mean absolute error from

00:01:24.975 --> 00:01:27.185
about ninety four to twenty six.

00:01:27.795 --> 00:01:30.775
It also improves this
depth sweeps later error,

00:01:30.775 --> 00:01:33.825
from about one hundred and
forty one to one hundred.

00:01:34.955 --> 00:01:38.985
Calling that sweep alone proof of
overfitting would be inaccurate.

00:01:39.715 --> 00:01:41.265
Both measured errors improved.

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.665
Compare the grouped baseline instead:

00:01:45.215 --> 00:01:47.455
it fits the training sample less closely,

00:01:47.455 --> 00:01:51.405
but scores better on later hours
at ninety one point five seven.

00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:56.095
Lower training error is therefore
insufficient to choose the function we

00:01:56.095 --> 00:01:57.065
carry forward.

00:01:59.358 --> 00:02:03.768
Now follow Monday, January ninth,
twenty twelve, at eight,

00:02:03.938 --> 00:02:06.088
whose observed target is
four hundred and twenty.

00:02:07.238 --> 00:02:11.748
The actual deep tree first excludes
hour seventeen and eighteen,

00:02:12.058 --> 00:02:13.968
then takes its hour eight branch.

00:02:14.798 --> 00:02:17.668
Further tests include working day status,

00:02:18.038 --> 00:02:18.468
January

00:02:18.998 --> 00:02:19.728
and weekdays.

00:02:20.598 --> 00:02:23.328
The reached leaf contains
eight training observations.

00:02:24.418 --> 00:02:26.768
These are real saved
conditions and support,

00:02:27.058 --> 00:02:30.308
not an illustrative tree
drawn after seeing the target.

00:02:30.898 --> 00:02:33.348
The request supplies
only the calendar vector.

00:02:34.018 --> 00:02:38.358
The later count is absent from its route
and becomes relevant when we score the

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:39.388
returned value.

00:02:41.587 --> 00:02:44.297
The eight supporting
counts are shown here.

00:02:44.747 --> 00:02:47.337
They sum to one thousand,
four hundred and forty six.

00:02:48.487 --> 00:02:51.257
Dividing by eight gives the stored mean,

00:02:51.647 --> 00:02:55.137
one hundred and eighty point
seven five rentals per hour.

00:02:55.587 --> 00:02:57.837
Against the later target
of four hundred and twenty,

00:02:58.627 --> 00:03:02.537
the absolute error is two hundred
and thirty nine point two five.

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:08.147
The broader hour and working day baseline
predicts about three hundred and forty

00:03:08.147 --> 00:03:10.617
nine point five, much closer.

00:03:11.327 --> 00:03:16.167
Finer conditioning has captured historical
detail that does not help this particular

00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:17.277
later commute.

00:03:17.927 --> 00:03:21.797
The result does not identify a
single cause of changed demand.

00:03:23.858 --> 00:03:28.228
Change only the requested hour to
two in the morning on that Monday.

00:03:28.718 --> 00:03:34.128
The actual saved tree follows another path
and returns about six point five five.

00:03:34.578 --> 00:03:39.208
The observed target is five,
so this is a useful quiet hour forecast.

00:03:39.898 --> 00:03:45.498
That success coexists with the busy hour
failure and a worse overall score than the

00:03:45.498 --> 00:03:46.128
group model.

00:03:46.578 --> 00:03:50.208
We keep both examples and the
full validation population,

00:03:50.578 --> 00:03:54.168
rather than selecting whichever makes
the newest model look strongest.

00:03:54.718 --> 00:03:56.828
This is why support, path,

00:03:57.178 --> 00:04:00.588
training error and later error
all belong in the explanation.

